# Nothing under data/ is committed except the provenance manifest.
#
# Raw downloads are large and reproducible via `python -m data_prep.download`.
# interim/ and processed/ are derived from them -- and because the Nesta
# suitability data is CC BY-NC-SA, committing those derivatives would impose a
# non-commercial share-alike licence on the repository. Worth an explicit
# decision before any of it is checked in.
#
# MANIFEST.md IS committed: it records the URL, licence, retrieval date and
# SHA256 of every snapshot, which is what makes a past result reproducible once
# the upstream files move (Nesta are retiring the suitability dataset).
# That decision has now been taken for three files, and only three. They are a
# few KB each and are committed because DESIGN.md quotes their numbers: a
# design note whose figures cannot be checked without a large download and a
# clustering run is not really reviewable. The per-LSOA assignments file stays
# out -- 33,755 rows is a data release, not evidence for an argument -- and so
# does lsoa_table.csv, which carries the Nesta columns outright.
#
# Licence check on the three: diagnostics and study_towns are functions of
# Census 2021 and IMD 2025 alone, both OGL. The profiles file is too, with one
# exception -- its `pct_urban` column comes from `rural_urban_class`, which
# reaches the table through the Nesta workbook rather than from ONS directly.
# The underlying rural/urban classification is ONS OGL data that Nesta
# redistribute, so carrying an aggregate of it should not pull the CC BY-NC-SA
# share-alike condition across, but that is a judgement rather than a settled
# reading. Sourcing the classification from ONS directly would remove the
# question entirely; see the Nesta open question in DESIGN.md.
raw/*
!raw/MANIFEST.md
interim/
processed/*
!processed/lsoa_types_profiles.csv
!processed/lsoa_types_diagnostics.csv
!processed/lsoa_types_study_towns.csv
